WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.440
This tissue is responding at its maximum.

2
00:00:06.980 --> 00:00:10.720
Yet only about five percent of its receptors are occupied.

3
00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:13.920
The other ninety-five percent are empty.

4
00:00:15.020 --> 00:00:19.480
How can a tissue give a full response with almost all of its receptors empty?

5
00:00:20.283 --> 00:00:22.803
To see why, start with binding.

6
00:00:23.703 --> 00:00:26.863
A ligand is any molecule that binds the receptor.

7
00:00:27.723 --> 00:00:33.923
The fraction of receptors occupied is the ligand concentration, divided by Kd plus

8
00:00:33.923 --> 00:00:35.203
the ligand concentration.

9
00:00:36.223 --> 00:00:41.643
Kd is the dissociation constant, the ligand concentration that fills half of the

10
00:00:41.643 --> 00:00:47.903
receptors. 5% occupancy comes at a ligand concentration far below Kd.

11
00:00:48.683 --> 00:00:53.123
Alfred Clark assumed that response is proportional to occupancy.

12
00:00:54.043 --> 00:00:58.643
The EC50 is the concentration that gives half of the maximum response.

13
00:00:59.663 --> 00:01:03.123
With that assumption, the EC50 would equal Kd.

14
00:01:04.163 --> 00:01:10.023
But in 1956, Mark Nickerson reported that a tissue can reach its full response

15
00:01:10.023 --> 00:01:12.443
before all of its receptors are occupied.

16
00:01:13.367 --> 00:01:18.007
The reason is amplification in the pathway after the receptor.

17
00:01:19.007 --> 00:01:22.227
Picture a bottling line where the capper is the bottleneck.

18
00:01:23.047 --> 00:01:27.127
Once it runs at full speed, extra filling machines add nothing.

19
00:01:28.007 --> 00:01:32.947
Likewise, once enough receptors are occupied to saturate the slowest step

20
00:01:32.947 --> 00:01:36.387
downstream, more occupied receptors change nothing.

21
00:01:37.307 --> 00:01:40.807
The receptors that are not needed are called spare receptors.

22
00:01:40.807 --> 00:01:43.727
Together they make up the receptor reserve.

23
00:01:44.767 --> 00:01:48.547
Kd is a property of the drug and its receptor.

24
00:01:49.367 --> 00:01:52.467
The EC50 is a property of the whole pathway.

25
00:01:53.567 --> 00:01:59.067
Say the pathway reaches its ceiling at 5% occupancy, and responds in proportion

26
00:01:59.067 --> 00:02:04.527
below that. Half of the maximum then needs only 2.5% occupancy.

27
00:02:04.527 --> 00:02:10.847
That puts the EC50 39 times below Kd, with no change in binding.

28
00:02:11.767 --> 00:02:17.047
You can prove a reserve by taking receptors away with an irreversible blocker.

29
00:02:17.687 --> 00:02:23.187
Remove half, and the curve moves right; remove 90%, and it moves further right.

30
00:02:23.987 --> 00:02:29.007
In this tissue, only when more than 95% are gone does the maximum fall.

31
00:02:29.827 --> 00:02:35.767
A real tissue has no sharp threshold, so the maximum sags gradually, but the order

32
00:02:35.767 --> 00:02:38.487
holds: shift first, fall later.

33
00:02:39.147 --> 00:02:44.267
Keep the rule: a full response does not mean every receptor is occupied.
